1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,780
Imagine someone tells you: “God exists.”

2
00:00:03,659 --> 00:00:06,079
Okay. So you ask: “How do you know?”

3
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:09,680
And they give you an answer.
“Because I have proof.”

4
00:00:10,165 --> 00:00:14,625
That sounds simple. But what would
that proof actually have to prove?

5
00:00:15,783 --> 00:00:21,043
And what if someone else told you: “God
does not exist.” Could they prove that?

6
00:00:21,662 --> 00:00:24,482
Could we ever definitively
prove either one?

7
00:00:25,086 --> 00:00:28,646
Maybe the problem is deeper than
the question of whether God exists.

8
00:00:29,186 --> 00:00:33,026
Maybe we first need to figure out
what it would even mean to prove it.

9
00:00:35,526 --> 00:00:40,586
Suppose I tell you there is an apple
inside this box. We can open it.

10
00:00:40,871 --> 00:00:43,791
If there is an apple
inside, we can observe it.

11
00:00:44,371 --> 00:00:48,091
We can photograph it, weigh it,
and let other people examine it.

12
00:00:48,531 --> 00:00:50,071
It's a claim we can test.

13
00:00:50,827 --> 00:00:53,067
Mathematical proof works differently.

14
00:00:53,707 --> 00:00:55,507
We don't have to observe anything.

15
00:00:56,258 --> 00:00:59,238
We can start with definitions
and rules, and logically

16
00:00:59,468 --> 00:01:02,558
demonstrate that a particular
conclusion must follow.

17
00:01:03,181 --> 00:01:05,221
Science works differently again.

18
00:01:05,921 --> 00:01:09,621
A scientific theory makes predictions
that can be compared with reality.

19
00:01:10,104 --> 00:01:12,384
And ideally, there should
be some possible observation

20
00:01:12,614 --> 00:01:15,124
that could show us that
the theory is wrong.

21
00:01:17,894 --> 00:01:20,994
And this is where the question
of God becomes difficult.

22
00:01:21,074 --> 00:01:24,214
Because there isn't
just one concept of God.

23
00:01:24,883 --> 00:01:27,243
Some people imagine a God
who interacts with the

24
00:01:27,473 --> 00:01:30,823
physical world, answers
prayers, and performs miracles.

25
00:01:31,699 --> 00:01:35,619
Others understand God as
something outside space and time.

26
00:01:36,419 --> 00:01:40,759
And some philosophers describe God as
the foundation of existence itself.

27
00:01:41,439 --> 00:01:43,839
These are not exactly the same hypothesis.

28
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:48,039
So we can't simply say:
“Science hasn't found God.”

29
00:01:48,727 --> 00:01:52,727
We first have to ask: What exactly
are we supposed to look for?

30
00:01:53,724 --> 00:01:57,204
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

31
00:01:57,824 --> 00:01:59,424
It really helps the channel.

32
00:02:01,814 --> 00:02:05,734
For centuries, philosophers have
offered arguments for God's existence.

33
00:02:06,603 --> 00:02:08,603
Take the cosmological argument.

34
00:02:09,123 --> 00:02:10,763
It starts with two premises.

35
00:02:11,603 --> 00:02:13,423
The universe began to exist.

36
00:02:13,913 --> 00:02:16,773
And everything that begins
to exist has a cause.

37
00:02:17,393 --> 00:02:18,913
So what caused the universe?

38
00:02:19,528 --> 00:02:22,088
Perhaps something outside
the universe itself.

39
00:02:22,188 --> 00:02:25,028
And that first cause could be called God.

40
00:02:25,570 --> 00:02:27,710
But notice something important.

41
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:32,367
Even if we accept that the universe has
a first cause, it doesn't automatically

42
00:02:32,597 --> 00:02:37,127
follow that this cause is a personal,
all-powerful, perfectly good God.

43
00:02:37,727 --> 00:02:39,427
That's another assumption.

44
00:02:40,799 --> 00:02:44,799
And the same problem appears in
other arguments. Take fine-tuning.

45
00:02:46,145 --> 00:02:49,125
Some physical constants have
values that allow stars,

46
00:02:49,205 --> 00:02:51,945
chemistry, planets, and
eventually life to exist. Why?

47
00:02:54,214 --> 00:02:57,534
One possibility is that it's
simply the result of chance.

48
00:02:58,363 --> 00:03:01,563
Another is that there are enormous
numbers of universes with different

49
00:03:01,793 --> 00:03:06,143
physical parameters, and we happen to
live in one that permits observers.

50
00:03:06,814 --> 00:03:09,154
Or perhaps a deeper law
we haven't discovered

51
00:03:09,234 --> 00:03:11,534
yet makes these values unavoidable.

52
00:03:12,171 --> 00:03:15,711
And another possibility is
that the universe was designed.

53
00:03:16,334 --> 00:03:20,414
But again: The observation itself
doesn't say: “God did this.”

54
00:03:20,734 --> 00:03:23,174
That's an interpretation
of the observation.

55
00:03:23,727 --> 00:03:27,427
That doesn't necessarily make the
philosophical argument worthless.

56
00:03:28,287 --> 00:03:32,507
It simply means that an argument is not
the same thing as an empirical proof.

57
00:03:35,237 --> 00:03:38,757
Now we're getting closer to
something we could potentially test.

58
00:03:39,493 --> 00:03:44,033
Suppose someone says: “God healed a
person who had an incurable disease.”

59
00:03:44,901 --> 00:03:47,181
We can examine the medical records.

60
00:03:47,781 --> 00:03:49,941
We can look for alternative explanations.

61
00:03:50,951 --> 00:03:54,491
We can investigate whether similar
cases occur without prayer.

62
00:03:55,311 --> 00:03:57,811
We can compare evidence
from different cases.

63
00:03:58,301 --> 00:04:00,501
If we see something we
can't explain, all we

64
00:04:00,731 --> 00:04:03,721
can conclude is: We don't
know what caused it.

65
00:04:04,242 --> 00:04:06,522
History is full of things
people once considered

66
00:04:06,752 --> 00:04:10,542
mysterious that later
received natural explanations.

67
00:04:11,399 --> 00:04:13,519
But the reverse is also important.

68
00:04:14,459 --> 00:04:16,819
If science gives us a
natural explanation for

69
00:04:17,049 --> 00:04:20,139
something, that doesn't
prove that God doesn't exist.

70
00:04:20,969 --> 00:04:25,469
“There's a natural explanation” is not
the same statement as “God doesn't exist.”

71
00:04:28,459 --> 00:04:30,239
Maybe this should be easier.

72
00:04:30,859 --> 00:04:34,599
We just need to find something that
makes God's existence impossible.

73
00:04:35,084 --> 00:04:36,664
But again: Which God?

74
00:04:37,727 --> 00:04:41,827
Suppose someone claims that God is
all-powerful and perfectly good.

75
00:04:42,527 --> 00:04:44,767
Now we encounter the problem of evil.

76
00:04:45,398 --> 00:04:50,958
The world contains enormous amounts of
suffering. Wars. Diseases. Earthquakes.

77
00:04:51,038 --> 00:04:52,758
Children dying from illnesses.

78
00:04:53,281 --> 00:04:57,521
If an all-powerful and perfectly good
God exists, why does this happen?

79
00:04:58,113 --> 00:05:00,613
This is a serious philosophical problem.

80
00:05:01,193 --> 00:05:05,693
A believer can propose different
explanations. Free will.

81
00:05:06,373 --> 00:05:09,913
The possibility that suffering is
necessary for some greater good.

82
00:05:10,482 --> 00:05:12,462
The limitations of human knowledge.

83
00:05:12,542 --> 00:05:15,462
Or the possibility that
our understanding of what

84
00:05:15,692 --> 00:05:18,362
is good is fundamentally
different from God's.

85
00:05:19,135 --> 00:05:22,455
We might find these
explanations unconvincing.

86
00:05:22,535 --> 00:05:25,315
We might think they fail
to solve the problem.

87
00:05:25,674 --> 00:05:29,834
But if we want to be precise, we have to
distinguish between: “This explanation

88
00:05:30,064 --> 00:05:34,414
doesn't convince me” and “This
explanation is logically impossible.”

89
00:05:35,221 --> 00:05:36,981
Those are very different claims.

90
00:05:39,701 --> 00:05:43,761
There is another interesting
problem. Suppose God exists.

91
00:05:44,341 --> 00:05:48,341
And suppose God does not want his
existence to be completely obvious.

92
00:05:48,895 --> 00:05:52,595
Then perhaps God could create a world
where there are reasons to believe...

93
00:05:52,815 --> 00:05:54,455
but never conclusive proof.

94
00:05:54,899 --> 00:05:57,859
And this leads to the
problem of divine hiddenness.

95
00:05:58,629 --> 00:06:00,569
If there is a God who wants humans to know

96
00:06:00,799 --> 00:06:03,789
him, why is it so unclear
whether he exists?

97
00:06:04,613 --> 00:06:09,113
Why are there people who genuinely
search for God and remain unconvinced?

98
00:06:09,295 --> 00:06:11,575
And why are there so
many different religions,

99
00:06:11,655 --> 00:06:14,275
making different claims
about what God wants?

100
00:06:14,959 --> 00:06:16,839
These are legitimate questions.

101
00:06:17,499 --> 00:06:19,699
They may simply mean that
a particular concept of

102
00:06:19,929 --> 00:06:22,819
God has difficulty explaining
the world we observe.

103
00:06:25,679 --> 00:06:29,479
What if we define God in a way that
makes him impossible to observe?

104
00:06:30,319 --> 00:06:34,399
Imagine a being that: doesn't
exist in space, doesn't exist in

105
00:06:34,629 --> 00:06:39,259
time, doesn't obey physical laws,
leaves no measurable traces, and

106
00:06:39,489 --> 00:06:42,939
whose existence cannot possibly
be disproved by any observation.

107
00:06:43,812 --> 00:06:47,852
Can we scientifically search
for such a being? Not really.

108
00:06:48,318 --> 00:06:50,838
But there's an even more
interesting question.

109
00:06:51,350 --> 00:06:55,590
If no possible observation could ever
show that such a being doesn't exist...

110
00:06:56,030 --> 00:06:58,390
what would it mean to
claim that it does exist?

111
00:06:59,747 --> 00:07:03,467
This is similar to a famous
thought experiment by Carl Sagan.

112
00:07:04,174 --> 00:07:08,254
Imagine I tell you: “There is an
invisible dragon in my garage.”

113
00:07:09,227 --> 00:07:13,847
You can't see it. You can't hear it. You
can't weigh it. It leaves no footprints.

114
00:07:14,423 --> 00:07:15,863
And whenever you bring in a scientific

115
00:07:16,093 --> 00:07:19,563
instrument, the dragon
somehow avoids detection.

116
00:07:20,364 --> 00:07:24,444
How could you prove that the
dragon isn't there? You couldn't.

117
00:07:25,006 --> 00:07:27,386
But that doesn't mean the dragon is there.

118
00:07:27,746 --> 00:07:31,346
And this illustrates a very
important principle of reasoning: The

119
00:07:31,576 --> 00:07:35,286
inability to disprove a claim is
not evidence that the claim is true.

120
00:07:38,396 --> 00:07:40,716
Maybe there are three different questions.

121
00:07:41,521 --> 00:07:43,781
First: Can we prove that God exists?

122
00:07:44,447 --> 00:07:48,787
For particular concepts of God, we
can construct philosophical arguments,

123
00:07:48,867 --> 00:07:53,107
historical arguments, or claims
based on alleged empirical evidence.

124
00:07:53,629 --> 00:07:57,809
But we don't have a universal,
indisputable proof of God's existence.

125
00:07:58,632 --> 00:08:01,372
Second: Can we prove
that God doesn't exist?

126
00:08:01,992 --> 00:08:03,652
That can be just as difficult.

127
00:08:04,169 --> 00:08:07,629
If God is defined in a way that
is compatible with every possible

128
00:08:07,859 --> 00:08:12,609
observation, there is no experiment
that could definitively rule him out.

129
00:08:12,903 --> 00:08:16,503
And third: Can we even create a
test that would decide the question?

130
00:08:17,045 --> 00:08:20,225
And here, everything depends
on how God is defined.

131
00:08:20,766 --> 00:08:23,586
If God interacts with the
physical world in specific,

132
00:08:23,666 --> 00:08:27,626
predictable ways, some claims
about God may be testable.

133
00:08:27,858 --> 00:08:30,778
But if God is defined as
something fundamentally beyond any

134
00:08:31,008 --> 00:08:35,078
possible observation, science has
no way to test the hypothesis.

135
00:08:37,938 --> 00:08:40,718
And this is where people
often make a mistake.

136
00:08:41,490 --> 00:08:45,970
An atheist might say: “You don't have
proof, therefore God doesn't exist.”

137
00:08:46,791 --> 00:08:49,471
A believer might respond:
“You can't prove that

138
00:08:49,701 --> 00:08:52,391
God doesn't exist,
therefore God does exist.”

139
00:08:53,223 --> 00:08:55,523
Neither argument works by itself.

140
00:08:56,391 --> 00:08:58,291
The first would be wrong
if it simply treated

141
00:08:58,521 --> 00:09:01,211
the absence of evidence
as proof of absence.

142
00:09:01,670 --> 00:09:04,570
But if a particular kind of God
should produce evidence that we would

143
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:08,710
reasonably expect to find — and
that evidence consistently isn't

144
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:12,930
there — then its absence can be
evidence against that hypothesis.

145
00:09:13,905 --> 00:09:16,285
The important point is
that this still doesn't

146
00:09:16,515 --> 00:09:19,025
automatically prove
that God does not exist.

147
00:09:19,782 --> 00:09:22,422
The second makes the
opposite mistake: treating

148
00:09:22,652 --> 00:09:25,602
the absence of disproof
as evidence of existence.

149
00:09:26,449 --> 00:09:31,409
It is perfectly possible to say: “I don't
have sufficient reason to believe this.”

150
00:09:31,569 --> 00:09:35,449
while also saying: “I don't have
proof that it is impossible.”

151
00:09:36,296 --> 00:09:37,876
There is no contradiction.

152
00:09:38,476 --> 00:09:41,476
It's simply an acknowledgment
of the limits of what we know.

153
00:09:44,206 --> 00:09:46,766
At the beginning, we
wanted a simple answer.

154
00:09:47,523 --> 00:09:49,803
Does God exist? Yes. Or no.

155
00:09:50,478 --> 00:09:54,238
But perhaps the evidence doesn't
let us give such a simple answer.

156
00:09:55,062 --> 00:09:56,742
We can study the universe.

157
00:09:57,262 --> 00:09:59,142
We can study the human brain.

158
00:09:59,662 --> 00:10:01,882
We can investigate alleged miracles.

159
00:10:02,693 --> 00:10:05,273
We can analyse philosophical arguments.

160
00:10:05,793 --> 00:10:07,633
We can compare different religions.

161
00:10:08,165 --> 00:10:10,005
And we can ask what we
would actually expect

162
00:10:10,235 --> 00:10:13,325
to observe if a particular
kind of God existed.

163
00:10:14,043 --> 00:10:18,283
But eventually, we reach a boundary
between claims that can be tested and

164
00:10:18,513 --> 00:10:23,083
claims that depend on philosophical
interpretation or personal belief.

165
00:10:24,096 --> 00:10:28,336
And perhaps the most intellectually
honest answer isn't: “God exists.”

166
00:10:28,496 --> 00:10:30,376
Or: “God doesn't exist.”

167
00:10:31,104 --> 00:10:35,004
It's this: It depends on what we mean
by God — and whether we've defined

168
00:10:35,234 --> 00:10:39,484
the claim in a way that reality
can actually confirm or disprove.

169
00:10:40,083 --> 00:10:43,563
Because sometimes the hardest
part isn't finding the answer.

170
00:10:44,496 --> 00:10:47,626
It's figuring out whether the
question can have an answer at all.
